/* Short-answer prompts: { id, topic, qhtml, answer }. Generated by build.py from content/practice*.md. */
window.SHORT_BANK = [
  {"id": "s-practice-1", "topic": "method", "qhtml": "<p><code>mpi_hello.c</code> is run with <code>mpiexec -n 1 ./mpi_hello</code>. What does it print, and how many times does the loop body on line 52 run?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It prints <code>Greetings from process 0 of 1!</code> and nothing else (run)</li>\n<li>Rank 0 is the only process, so it prints its own greeting</li>\n<li>The loop <code>for (int q = 1; q &lt; comm_sz; q++)</code> starts at 1 and <code>comm_sz</code> is 1, so the body runs zero times</li>\n<li>No sends or receives happen at all</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-2", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p><code>mpi_hello.c</code> runs on 8 processes. How many <code>MPI_Send</code> calls and how many <code>MPI_Recv</code> calls happen in total, and which ranks make them?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>7 sends, made by ranks 1 to 7, one each</li>\n<li>7 receives, all made by rank 0</li>\n<li>In general <code>comm_sz - 1</code> of each, and they must match one to one</li>\n<li>A receive with no send hangs, and a send with no receive can hang or lose the message</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-3", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>In <code>mpi_hello.c</code> the receive on line 54 uses <code>q</code> as the source. Suppose you change it to <code>MPI_ANY_SOURCE</code>. Does the program still work, and what can change in the output?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It still works: rank 0 still receives exactly 7 messages (for 8 processes) and prints each one</li>\n<li>The order of the greeting lines can now change from run to run</li>\n<li>With <code>q</code>, rank 0 waits for rank 1 first, then 2, and so on, which forces rank order</li>\n<li>With <code>MPI_ANY_SOURCE</code>, rank 0 takes whichever message arrives first</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-4", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>In <code>mpi_hello2.c</code>, rank 7 of 16 sends the string <code>\"Greetings from process 7 of 16!\"</code>. What does <code>MPI_Get_count</code> give rank 0 for that message?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The string has 31 characters</li>\n<li><code>strlen(greeting)+1</code> adds the terminator, so 32 elements are sent as <code>MPI_CHAR</code></li>\n<li><code>MPI_Get_count(&amp;status, MPI_CHAR, &amp;count)</code> therefore sets <code>count</code> to 32</li>\n<li>Check with the real run on 4 ranks: \"Greetings from process 1 of 4!\" is 30 characters and rank 0 reported 31 elements</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-5", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>A receiver uses <code>MPI_ANY_SOURCE</code> and <code>MPI_ANY_TAG</code>. How does it find out who sent the message, what tag it had, and how many elements arrived?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Pass the address of an <code>MPI_Status</code> variable as the last argument of <code>MPI_Recv</code></li>\n<li><code>status.MPI_SOURCE</code> holds the sender's rank</li>\n<li><code>status.MPI_TAG</code> holds the tag</li>\n<li><code>MPI_Get_count(&amp;status, datatype, &amp;count)</code> gives the number of elements</li>\n<li>The count is a function call because it depends on the datatype</li>\n<li>Only a receiver can use wildcards, a sender must always name a destination and a tag</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-6", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>What happens when this program runs on 2 processes?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Send</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Recv</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_STATUS_IGNORE</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">                 </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"got %d</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"p\">);</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It hangs and never prints (run: it had to be killed after a timeout)</li>\n<li>The send uses tag 5 and the receive wants tag 7, so they do not match</li>\n<li>Rank 0 blocks forever waiting for a message that matches</li>\n<li>Rank 1's message has no receive, so it is never delivered</li>\n<li>With <code>MPI_ANY_TAG</code> on the receive, the pair would match</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-7", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>Two processes each run this code, where <code>partner</code> is the other rank. Is it safe?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Ssend</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partner</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Recv</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">other</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partner</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_STATUS_IGNORE</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>No. Both ranks block in <code>MPI_Ssend</code>, each waiting for the other to start a receive, so neither ever gets to its <code>MPI_Recv</code> (run: it hangs)</li>\n<li>This is a deadlock, because each process waits for an event the other can only cause after it finishes waiting</li>\n<li>With plain <code>MPI_Send</code> it may seem to work if the message is small enough to be buffered, which is exactly why it is called unsafe</li>\n<li>Safety check: swap <code>MPI_Send</code> for <code>MPI_Ssend</code>, if it hangs it is unsafe</li>\n<li>Fix one: even ranks send then receive, odd ranks receive then send</li>\n<li>Fix two: use <code>MPI_Sendrecv</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-8", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>Three processes all have <code>a = 1</code> and <code>c = 2</code>. Rank 1 executes the first block below and ranks 0 and 2 execute the second. What are <code>b</code> and <code>d</code> on rank 0?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"cm\">/* rank 1 */</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n\n<span class=\"cm\">/* ranks 0 and 2 */</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>b = 4</code> and <code>d = 5</code> (run)</li>\n<li>Collective calls match by the order they are made, not by the variable names</li>\n<li>The first call on every rank combines <code>a</code> (rank 0) + <code>c</code> (rank 1) + <code>a</code> (rank 2) = 1 + 2 + 1 = 4, and that goes into <code>b</code> on rank 0</li>\n<li>The second call combines 2 + 1 + 2 = 5 and goes into <code>d</code></li>\n<li>This is textbook Table 3.3</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-9", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>What is wrong with this call?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_DOUBLE</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The input and output buffers are the same variable, which is called <strong>aliasing</strong></li>\n<li>MPI forbids aliasing an output argument, so the call is illegal</li>\n<li>The result is unpredictable: it might be correct, wrong, or crash</li>\n<li>Use two separate variables, as in <code>MPI_Reduce(&amp;local_int, &amp;total_int, ...)</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-10", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>Four ranks each set <code>mine = rank + 1</code> and <code>total = -1</code>, then run both calls below. What do rank 0 and rank 1 hold in <code>total</code> and <code>total2</code> afterwards?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Reduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">total</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Allreduce</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">total2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_SUM</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Rank 0: <code>total = 10</code> and <code>total2 = 10</code></li>\n<li>Rank 1: <code>total = -1</code> and <code>total2 = 10</code> (run)</li>\n<li>1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</li>\n<li><code>MPI_Reduce</code> only writes its output on the destination rank, so rank 1's <code>total</code> is untouched</li>\n<li><code>MPI_Allreduce</code> has no destination, every rank gets the result</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-11", "topic": "trapezoid", "qhtml": "<p><code>mpi_Bcast.c</code> is run with <code>echo \"0 3 1024\" | mpiexec -n 3 ./mpi_Bcast</code>. The true integral of x squared from 0 to 3 is 9. What is wrong with the answer?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It prints about 8.9737 (run: 8.973662840668112e+00) with no error message</li>\n<li><code>local_n = n/comm_sz</code> is integer division, so 1024/3 is 341</li>\n<li>3 ranks times 341 is 1023 trapezoids, so the last strip of the interval is never computed</li>\n<li>The program assumes <code>n</code> is divisible by <code>comm_sz</code></li>\n<li>It gives the same correct-looking answer on 1, 2, 4 and 8 ranks (run), because they all divide 1024</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-12", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>Find the bug.</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">Get_input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_rank</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">comm_sz</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">double</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">double</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n_p</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">my_rank</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Enter a, b, and n</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">scanf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"%lf %lf %d\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n_p</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Bcast</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_DOUBLE</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Bcast</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_DOUBLE</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Bcast</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n_p</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The <code>MPI_Bcast</code> calls are inside the <code>if</code>, so only rank 0 makes them</li>\n<li>Every process in the communicator must call a collective function, so this is erroneous</li>\n<li>The other ranks never receive <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>n</code> and compute with uninitialized values</li>\n<li>Fix: close the <code>if</code> after <code>scanf</code>, so all ranks execute the three <code>MPI_Bcast</code> calls</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-13", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>A sum over 8 processes is done two ways: rank 0 loops over <code>MPI_Recv</code> from each other rank, or <code>MPI_Reduce</code> uses a tree. How many steps does rank 0 take in each?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The loop: 7 receives, one after another</li>\n<li>The tree: 3 rounds, which is log2 of 8</li>\n<li>Round 1: ranks 1, 3, 5, 7 send to 0, 2, 4, 6</li>\n<li>Round 2: ranks 2 and 6 send to 0 and 4</li>\n<li>Round 3: rank 4 sends to 0</li>\n<li>The lecture's figure shows this shape, real implementations may choose a different tree</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-14", "topic": "collectives", "qhtml": "<p>Four ranks run this code. What does rank 2 hold in <code>mine</code> right after the scatter, and what does rank 0 print at the end?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">all</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">},</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">],</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">back</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">};</span>\n<span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">8</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">size</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\">                       </span><span class=\"cm\">/* size is 4 */</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Scatter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">all</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">++</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Gather</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">mine</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">back</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_COMM_WORLD</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"cm\">/* rank 0 then prints back[0] to back[7] */</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>chunk</code> is 2, and <code>send_count</code> is the number of elements per process</li>\n<li>Rank 2 gets the third chunk: <code>mine[0] = 5</code> and <code>mine[1] = 6</code></li>\n<li>Each rank multiplies its chunk by 10</li>\n<li>Gather puts rank 0's chunk first, then rank 1's, and so on</li>\n<li>Rank 0 prints <code>10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80</code> (run)</li>\n<li>Passing 8 as the send count instead of <code>chunk</code> makes MPI abort with \"Message truncated\" (run)</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-15", "topic": "trapezoid", "qhtml": "<p><code>mpi_SendRec.c</code> runs on 6 processes. How many point-to-point messages travel in total, and where do they go?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Input: 3 sends to each of the 5 other ranks, which is 15</li>\n<li>Results: 1 message from each of the 5 other ranks, which is 5</li>\n<li>Total 20, which is 4(p - 1) with p = 6</li>\n<li>All 20 are sent to or from rank 0</li>\n<li>The <code>mpi_Bcast.c</code> version does the same job with 3 <code>MPI_Bcast</code> calls and 1 <code>MPI_Reduce</code> call on every rank</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice-16", "topic": "sendrecv", "qhtml": "<p>Rank 1 calls <code>MPI_Send</code> with 10 ints and tag 3. Rank 0 calls <code>MPI_Recv</code> for source 1, tag 3, same communicator and datatype, but a count of 5. Will they match?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The source, destination, tag and communicator all match</li>\n<li>But the buffers are not compatible, because the textbook's rule of thumb needs the receive count to be at least the send count</li>\n<li>The receive buffer is too small for the message, so MPI aborts with a \"Message truncated\" error (run)</li>\n<li>A receive count larger than the send count is fine, which is why <code>mpi_hello.c</code> receives with <code>MAX_STRING</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-1", "topic": "odd_even", "qhtml": "<p>Odd-even sort, 4 ranks. In an <strong>odd</strong> phase, who is rank 0's partner, and what does rank 0 do?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Rank 0 is even, and in an odd phase an even rank's partner is rank - 1 = -1</li>\n<li>-1 is not a valid rank, so rank 0 has no partner: no <code>MPI_Sendrecv</code>, no merge</li>\n<li>It moves straight on to the next phase without waiting for anyone</li>\n<li>Rank 3 is idle too: it is odd, so its partner is 3 + 1 = 4, which is past the last rank</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-2", "topic": "odd_even", "qhtml": "<p>Ranks 2 and 3 hold the sorted blocks <code>12 21 27 49</code> and <code>6 9 18 40</code> and do a compare-split in an even phase. What does each keep?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Merge the two blocks: <code>6 9 12 18 21 27 40 49</code></li>\n<li>The lower rank keeps the low half: rank 2 gets <code>6 9 12 18</code></li>\n<li>The higher rank keeps the high half: rank 3 gets <code>21 27 40 49</code></li>\n<li>In the code, rank 3 (odd) calls <code>Merge_keep_high</code> and rank 2 calls <code>Merge_keep_low</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-3", "topic": "odd_even", "qhtml": "<p>The pthreads odd-even sort needed two barriers per phase. Why does the MPI version need none?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>In pthreads, all blocks sit in one shared address space, so a fast thread could overwrite its block while a slow partner was still reading it</li>\n<li>In MPI each rank's memory is private. A partner only ever sees the copy it received, so there is nothing to race over</li>\n<li><code>MPI_Sendrecv</code> synchronizes the two ranks of a pair as a side effect of moving the data</li>\n<li>Ranks without a partner make no call and do not wait for anyone</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-4", "topic": "odd_even", "qhtml": "<p>Both ranks of a pair run this. It passes every test with 16 numbers. Is it correct?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Send</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">local_A</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">local_n</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partner</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">comm</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_Recv</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">temp_B</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">local_n</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n mpi\">MPI_INT</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">partner</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">comm</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;</span><span class=\"n\">status</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>No, it is unsafe</li>\n<li>Whether <code>MPI_Send</code> blocks is up to the implementation and usually depends on message size</li>\n<li>Small blocks get buffered, so the send returns before the partner posts a receive and the test passes</li>\n<li>Large blocks are not buffered, so both ranks wait in <code>MPI_Send</code> for a receive that never starts: deadlock</li>\n<li>Proof: change <code>MPI_Send</code> to <code>MPI_Ssend</code> and it hangs immediately</li>\n<li>Fix: <code>MPI_Sendrecv</code>, or have one rank of each pair receive first</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-5", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>Heat diffusion, N = 16, 4 ranks. Which global rows does rank 2 own, and which global rows are copied into its two ghost rows?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>14 updatable rows, <code>base = 3</code>, <code>rem = 2</code>, so the counts are 4, 4, 3, 3</li>\n<li><code>row_start</code> for rank 2 is 1 + 4 + 4 = 9, so it owns global rows 9, 10 and 11</li>\n<li>Top ghost (local row 0): a copy of global row 8, owned by rank 1</li>\n<li>Bottom ghost (local row 4): a copy of global row 12, owned by rank 3</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-6", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>Rank 2 above is at local row 3 and column j. Which global row does it pass to <code>isFixed</code>, and why does it matter?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>global_row = row_start - 1 + local_row = 9 - 1 + 3 = 11</code></li>\n<li><code>isFixed</code> asks about the plate (the cold edges and the hot block), and those are defined in global coordinates</li>\n<li>Passing the local row 3 would test the wrong row, so the hot block would land in the wrong place for that rank</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-7", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>Why does the heat program use <code>MPI_PROC_NULL</code> instead of an <code>if</code> around the halo exchange?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Rank 0 has no rank above it and the last rank has none below it</li>\n<li>A send or receive with <code>MPI_PROC_NULL</code> finishes immediately and does nothing</li>\n<li>The ghost row on that side keeps its initial value, which is the cold edge of the plate, which is correct</li>\n<li>So every rank can make the same two <code>MPI_Sendrecv</code> calls, with no special cases</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-8", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>Someone deletes the down-exchange <code>MPI_Sendrecv</code> (keeping only the up-exchange) and runs on 4 ranks. What happens?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It hangs on the very first iteration, before writing any frame (run: killed after a timeout)</li>\n<li>Rank 1's up-exchange sends to and receives from rank 0</li>\n<li>But rank 0's up-exchange is with <code>MPI_PROC_NULL</code>, and its down-exchange was deleted, so rank 0 never sends to rank 1</li>\n<li>Rank 1 waits forever for that row. Every up-exchange depends on a down-exchange that no longer exists</li>\n<li>The slides suggest a visible seam instead. With this file, removing either call on every rank leaves every pair unmatched, so it hangs</li>\n<li>On 1 rank it still runs, because both neighbours are <code>MPI_PROC_NULL</code> (run)</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-9", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>The halo exchange is moved to <strong>after</strong> the compute phase. Does the program still converge? Is it right?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It still converges, but the result is wrong (run on 4 ranks: 89 iterations instead of 69, and the frames differ)</li>\n<li>The compute phase now reads ghost rows from the previous iteration, which are stale</li>\n<li>So the cells next to a rank boundary use old neighbour values</li>\n<li>On 1 rank the result is still correct (run), because there are no ghost rows between ranks</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-10", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>The heat program uses <code>MPI_Gatherv</code> to build each frame. Why not <code>MPI_Gather</code>? With N = 16 and 4 ranks, what are <code>recv_counts[3]</code> and <code>displs[3]</code>?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>MPI_Gather</code> needs every rank to send the same amount, but the bands here are 4, 4, 3 and 3 rows</li>\n<li><code>MPI_Gatherv</code> takes a count and a displacement for each rank</li>\n<li>Rank 3: <code>row_count = 3</code>, <code>row_start = 12</code></li>\n<li><code>recv_counts[3] = 3 * 16 = 48</code> and <code>displs[3] = 12 * 16 = 192</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-11", "topic": "heat", "qhtml": "<p>A rank finishes the compute phase very early and calls <code>MPI_Allreduce</code>. What happens to it, and what does that do to the measured times?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>It waits inside <code>MPI_Allreduce</code> until every rank has entered the call</li>\n<li>That waiting is counted in <code>reduce_time</code> (and waiting in the Sendrecvs in <code>comm_time</code>)</li>\n<li>So those timers mostly measure load imbalance, not data transfer. In the lecture, 0.466 s of \"comm\" was for 4 KB rows</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-12", "topic": "performance", "qhtml": "<p>A program's speedup on 8 cores is 6. What is its efficiency, and what does that number mean?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>E = S / p = 6 / 8 = 0.75</code></li>\n<li>On average each core spends 75% of its time doing useful work on the problem</li>\n<li>The rest is overhead: communication, synchronization, idle time</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-13", "topic": "performance", "qhtml": "<p>90% of a program can be parallelized perfectly. What speedup does Amdahl's law give on 4 cores, and what is the most it could ever reach?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li><code>S = 1 / (0.9/4 + 0.1) = 1 / 0.325 = 3.08</code> (the lecture table shows 3.07)</li>\n<li>The maximum, as p grows without limit, is <code>1 / 0.1 = 10</code></li>\n<li>Even with 1000 cores, the 10% serial part keeps it under 10</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-14", "topic": "performance", "qhtml": "<p>Classify each with Flynn's taxonomy: a single-core laptop from 2005, an x86 SSE vector unit, an MPI cluster, a 16-core desktop CPU.</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Single-core laptop: SISD</li>\n<li>SSE vector unit: SIMD</li>\n<li>MPI cluster: MIMD with distributed memory (each node is often shared memory, so hybrid)</li>\n<li>16-core desktop: MIMD with shared memory</li>\n<li>A GPU is usually described as SIMT, a lockstep relative of SIMD</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-15", "topic": "performance", "qhtml": "<p>A crossbar switch connects 4 CPUs to 4 memory modules. When do two CPUs slow each other down? How is that different from a bus?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Only when two CPUs want the same memory module at the same time</li>\n<li>Otherwise each CPU gets its own path, so all four can access memory at once</li>\n<li>On a bus every access shares one path, so CPUs wait for each other even when they want different memory</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-16", "topic": "gpgpu", "qhtml": "<p>What is wrong with this Warp kernel, meant to compute <code>z = x + y</code>?</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code>@wp.kernel\ndef addArrays(x: wp.array(dtype=float), y: wp.array(dtype=float)):\n    tid = wp.tid()\n    return x[tid] + y[tid]</code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Kernels do not return values, so the sums go nowhere</li>\n<li>Add an output parameter <code>z: wp.array(dtype=float)</code> and write <code>z[tid] = x[tid] + y[tid]</code></li>\n<li>On the host, create <code>z</code> with <code>wp.zeros(...)</code> and pass it in <code>outputs=[z]</code></li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-17", "topic": "gpgpu", "qhtml": "<p>A kernel contains <code>if x[tid] &gt; 0: ... else: ...</code>, and about half the threads in each group take each branch. Why is this slow on a GPU?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Threads in a group run in lockstep with one program counter</li>\n<li>When they disagree, the group runs the <code>if</code> path with the <code>else</code> threads masked off, then the <code>else</code> path with the <code>if</code> threads masked off</li>\n<li>The time is the sum of both branches, as if every thread ran both</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-18", "topic": "gpgpu", "qhtml": "<p>A GPU version of a small program (1000 elements) runs slower than the CPU version. Give the most likely reason.</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Copying the data from CPU memory to GPU memory and back goes over the bus, which is expensive</li>\n<li>For small problems that copy can take longer than the computation it saves</li>\n<li>The GPU only pays off when there is enough work per byte copied</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-19", "topic": "filters", "qhtml": "<p>Apply the 3 x 3 mean filter to the centre pixel. Then apply the high-pass kernel (8 in the centre, -1 around it) to the same neighbourhood.</p>\n<figure class=\"snippet\"><pre><code>10  10  10\n10  90  10\n10  10  10</code></pre></figure>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Mean: sum is 8 x 10 + 90 = 170, and 170 / 9 = 18.9</li>\n<li>High-pass: 8 x 90 - 8 x 10 = 720 - 80 = 640</li>\n<li>The mean filter spreads the bright spot out, the high-pass filter makes it stand out</li>\n<li>A median filter here gives 10: the bright pixel is an outlier and is removed completely</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-20", "topic": "imaging", "qhtml": "<p>A 5 x 5 kernel is used on a 100 x 80 image with no border handling. How big is the region where convolution is possible, and where does it start?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Half-width m = 2</li>\n<li>Region: (100 - 4) x (80 - 4) = 96 x 76</li>\n<li>It starts at (2, 2), the first pixel that is 2 away from every edge</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-21", "topic": "imaging", "qhtml": "<p>Why are smoothing kernels normalized, and why is the sharpening kernel's sum 0 instead of 1?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A smoothing kernel divided by its sum keeps a constant region at the same value and keeps total brightness unchanged</li>\n<li>The sharpening kernel is meant to give 0 on constant regions and a large value at edges, so its coefficients add to 0</li>\n<li>Its output is an edge map, which is then added back to the image (as in unsharp masking)</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-22", "topic": "filters", "qhtml": "<p>Unsharp masking with k = 0.5 is applied to the row <code>10 10 10 10 50 50 50 50</code>, with a 3-wide mean as the blur. What are the two values next to the edge, and what is this effect called?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Blur: <code>10 10 10 23.3 36.7 50 50 50</code></li>\n<li>Edge image g = f - blur: <code>0 0 0 -13.3 13.3 0 0 0</code></li>\n<li><code>f + 0.5 g</code>: <code>10 10 10 3.3 56.7 50 50 50</code></li>\n<li>The values next to the edge are 3.3 and 56.7: below 10 and above 50</li>\n<li>That is <strong>overshoot</strong>: the edge gets steeper by getting taller</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-23", "topic": "filters", "qhtml": "<p>An image has salt-and-pepper noise. Which filter do you choose, a 3 x 3 mean or a 3 x 3 median, and what is the risk?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The median: an isolated noisy pixel never ends up as the middle value, so it is removed, and edges stay sharp</li>\n<li>The mean would only smear each spike into its neighbours</li>\n<li>Risk: any real feature smaller than half the neighbourhood (a thin line, a small dot) is removed too</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-24", "topic": "filters", "qhtml": "<p>Is a median filter a convolution? Could you write it as a kernel matrix?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>No. Convolution is a weighted sum, and the median comes from sorting</li>\n<li>No fixed set of weights gives the middle value for every neighbourhood, so there is no kernel matrix</li>\n<li>It is a non-linear, order-statistic (rank) filter</li>\n</ul>"},
  {"id": "s-practice2-25", "topic": "filters", "qhtml": "<p>In a Gaussian filter, what happens when sigma increases?</p>", "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The weights spread out more, so more blur</li>\n<li>The kernel gets bigger, since it runs from -[3 sigma] to [3 sigma]</li>\n<li>It stays rotationally symmetric, so no direction is blurred more than another</li>\n</ul>"}
];
